[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says: one issue, one fix queued. Rendered, its own table says more.
Its reports hit more than one bug, and some of them are not a flipped date at all. This page
splits #4471 by problem. Each problem has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed, and the hint will not be fixed because
the booking-v3 form is retired. Neither change touches the one-day move. Its ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## The problems in #4471

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

## parser: day and month swap

The shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants write the day first, so a booking for
7 April goes out as 4 July. This is the bug #4471 was opened for. Engineering fixed it with
`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`, so its ticket is closed as completed.

## hint: the booking-v3 form asks for the month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. On a day-first tenant, people type what the
hint says, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The date flips, so it
looks like the parser bug, but the parser fix does not touch it. Engineering will not fix the hint:
the booking-v3 form is retired. Its ticket is closed as not planned.

## one-day move: the date moves by one day

These reports are not a swap. Tuesday shows as Monday. A client in Lisbon gets Tuesday and a
client in Toronto gets Monday for the same meeting. An invite arrives before the booking was made.
The desk read them as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`). They stay filed, and counted, under their
tenant's cause, as the desk counts, and they have a ticket of their own too. A day lost between time
zones does not come from the order of day and month: the parser fix and the retired form leave it
as it is, so this ticket stays open.

## How a report is placed

1. Its tenant writes the day first and it came through booking-v3's form: `hint`.
2. Its tenant writes the day first: `parser`.
3. No tenant on file: `unplaced`. It has no ticket; read it before you file it.
4. The desk read it as a one-day move: it is also a `one-day move`, and stays counted where it is
   filed.

The rules run in `src/lib/kb/problems.ts`, on top of the desk's `causes.ts`, and count the way
#4471's own table does. Intake's tag is shown, never used: it is a guess from the reporter's
browser, and the tenant decides the cause.

## While the desk keeps working

#4471 is closed. By subject ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), a new date
report now opens a new ticket or joins any open one with the same subject, and the subject does not
tell these problems apart. The table above counts again on every render. Before you file a new date
report, place it with the steps above or the [triage explorer](/kb/triage/dedupe). A one-day move
goes to its open ticket.

